// PIN ZYJS-001 — what the catch-all was hiding.
//
// `>> a >> b ¶` on one line is two output statements; both Rust engines break
// at the second `>>` (`parse_output`, io.rs). zyjs had no such rule and got the
// right answer anyway: the second `>>` fell through `parsePrimary`'s catch-all,
// came back as a Unit literal, and Unit prints as nothing.
//
// Removing the catch-all is what made the hole visible — 18 corpus files, all
// of them chained output, went red at once. The rule is implemented now rather
// than simulated. Closed 2026-08-30.
//
// A pin and not a corpus file: the corpus files that exercise this are about
// lambdas and pipes and happen to print this way, so a change here would be
// diagnosed as a lambda regression.
n = 5
>> "a: " >> n ¶
>> "x" >> "y" >> "z" ¶
